    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<p>As we all know, <a href="/product-category/sesame-series/">sesame</a> is one of the common food materials and ingredients in our lives. It is rich in nutrients and can be used for both culinary and medicinal purposes. In the market, we often encounter hulled sesame seeds vs unhulled sesame seeds. Whether for baking, cooking, or health benefits, sesame is an essential and delicious ingredient. But do you really understand the differences between shelled and unshelled sesame? Today, let&#8217;s delve into the characteristics and uses of both types of sesame!</p><figure id="attachment_565-2" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-565-2" style="width: 749px" class="wp-caption alignnone"><img src="/images/Hulled-Sesame-Seeds-vs-Unhulled-Sesame-Seeds-300x187.jpg" decoding="async" class=" wp-image-565" alt="Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds" width="759" height="473" title="Hulled Sesame Seeds VS Unhulled Sesame Seeds in 5 Aspects 5" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-565-2" class="wp-caption-text">Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds</figcaption></figure><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_85 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#What_do_Hulled_and_Unhulled_Mean" >What do Hulled and Unhulled Mean?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#Can_Both_White_and_Black_Sesame_Be_Made_into_Shelled_or_Unshelled_Products" >Can Both White and Black Sesame Be Made into Shelled or Unshelled Products?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#Is_Unhulled_White_Sesame_Better" >Is Unhulled White Sesame Better?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#Do_Hulled_and_Unshelled_Sesame_Have_the_Same_Nutritional_Content" >Do Hulled and Unshelled Sesame Have the Same Nutritional Content?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#Is_Shelled_Sesame_More_Expensive_Than_Unshelled_Sesame" >Is Shelled Sesame More Expensive Than Unshelled Sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#Unhulled_Black_Sesame_vs_Unhulled_White_Sesame_Which_is_Better" >Unhulled Black Sesame vs. Unhulled White Sesame: Which is Better?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#Which_is_Better_for_Industrial_Production_of_Sesame_Oil_Hulled_Sesame_Seeds_vs_Unhulled_Sesame_Seeds" >Which is Better for Industrial Production of Sesame Oil: Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds?</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_do_Hulled_and_Unhulled_Mean"></span>What do Hulled and Unhulled Mean?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>First, let’s clarify the definitions of “shelled” and “unshelled.” Shelled sesame, as the name suggests, still has its hard outer shell, which serves as a natural protective layer for the seeds during growth. Unshelled sesame has had this outer shell removed, leaving only the golden sesame seeds, which appear more enticing and have a finer texture.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Can_Both_White_and_Black_Sesame_Be_Made_into_Shelled_or_Unshelled_Products"></span>Can Both White and Black Sesame Be Made into Shelled or Unshelled Products?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The answer is yes! Both white and black sesame can be processed into either shelled or unshelled products. Generally, black sesame, due to its darker color, appears fuller when shelled, so many people prefer shelled black sesame. Thus, most black sesame products in the market are shelled. On the other hand, unshelled white sesame looks cleaner and neater, making it more suitable for direct consumption or dessert-making, so unshelled white sesame is also quite common in the market.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Is_Unhulled_White_Sesame_Better"></span>Is Unhulled White Sesame Better?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Both shelled and unshelled white sesame have their own merits; the choice mainly depends on your usage scenario and personal preference. Here are the advantages of each:</p><ul><li><strong>Shelled White Sesame:</strong> Retains the original flavor and nutrients of sesame; the shell can add texture and aroma during cooking. Additionally, shelled sesame is generally more stable during storage and less prone to moisture damage.</li><li><strong>Unshelled White Sesame:</strong> Has a finer texture, making it suitable for baking and making sauces where a delicate texture is desired. Unshelled sesame also releases its aroma more easily during cooking, enhancing the dish’s flavor.</li></ul><figure id="attachment_567-2" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-567-2" style="width: 757px" class="wp-caption alignnone"><img src="/images/Hulled-Sesame-Seeds-vs-Unhulled-Sesame-Seeds-1-300x193.jpg" decoding="async" class=" wp-image-567" alt="Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds" width="767" height="493" title="Hulled Sesame Seeds VS Unhulled Sesame Seeds in 5 Aspects 6" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-567-2" class="wp-caption-text">Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds</figcaption></figure><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Do_Hulled_and_Unshelled_Sesame_Have_the_Same_Nutritional_Content"></span>Do Hulled and Unshelled Sesame Have the Same Nutritional Content?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>From a nutritional perspective, there is no significant difference in the nutrient composition between shelled and unshelled sesame. Both are rich in protein, fats, dietary fiber, vitamins, and minerals. However, because shelled sesame retains the complete structure of the seeds, it may have slight advantages in certain trace elements and antioxidants.</p><table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;" border="1"><thead><tr><th>Nutritional Components</th><th>Hulled Sesame</th><th>Unhulled Sesame</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fats</td><td>High (about 40-60%)</td><td>High (about 50-55%)</td><td>One of sesame&#8217;s main components, mostly unsaturated fatty acids beneficial for cardiovascular health.</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Rich (about 17-25%)</td><td>Rich (about 20-25%)</td><td>Important nutrient, provides essential amino acids for the body.</td></tr><tr><td>Dietary Fiber</td><td>More (due to high fiber in the shell)</td><td>Less but still present</td><td>Dietary fiber helps promote gut health.</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamin E</td><td>Rich (high content)</td><td>Effective antioxidant, helps improve blood circulation and metabolism.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Minerals (e.g., Calcium, Iron)</td><td>Rich, contains calcium, iron, zinc, phosphorus, magnesium, etc.</td><td>Rich in various minerals beneficial for health.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Other Antioxidants (e.g., Sesamin)</td><td>Present and relatively high, such as sesamin, sesamolin, B vitamins, etc.</td><td>Present and relatively high, such as sesamin, sesamolin, anthocyanins, melanin, etc.</td><td>Helps reduce cholesterol and protect cardiovascular health.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>From the component table, we can see that the nutritional contents of shelled and unshelled sesame are quite similar, though there are differences in trace elements and other antioxidants.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Is_Shelled_Sesame_More_Expensive_Than_Unshelled_Sesame"></span>Is Shelled Sesame More Expensive Than Unshelled Sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>In terms of price, there is no fixed price difference between shelled and unshelled sesame. This mainly depends on market supply and demand, origin, variety, and processing costs. In certain areas or specific time periods, shelled sesame may be slightly cheaper due to lower processing costs. Conversely, unshelled sesame might be more popular due to its finer texture and convenience, potentially making it more expensive. Therefore, shelled sesame is not necessarily more expensive than unshelled sesame.</p><figure id="attachment_568-2" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-568-2" style="width: 766px" class="wp-caption alignnone"><img src="/images/Hulled-Sesame-Seeds-vs-Uehulled-Sesame-Seeds-2-1-300x198.jpg" decoding="async" class=" wp-image-568" alt="Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds" width="776" height="512" title="Hulled Sesame Seeds VS Unhulled Sesame Seeds in 5 Aspects 7" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-568-2" class="wp-caption-text">Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds</figcaption></figure><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Unhulled_Black_Sesame_vs_Unhulled_White_Sesame_Which_is_Better"></span>Unhulled Black Sesame vs. Unhulled White Sesame: Which is Better?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>When comparing unshelled black sesame and unshelled white sesame, their nutritional components are similar, but they differ in taste, uses, and health benefits.</p><table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;" border="1"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Unhulled White Sesame</th><th>Unhulled Black Sesame</th><th>Which is Better?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uses</td><td>Refreshing, suitable for desserts and sauces</td><td>Rich, with a unique aroma</td><td>Depends on personal taste preference</td></tr><tr><td>Taste</td><td>Ideal for baking and stir-frying</td><td>Suitable for traditional dishes like black sesame paste</td><td>Depends on cooking needs and purposes</td></tr><tr><td>Health Benefits</td><td>Rich in unsaturated fatty acids and vitamin E, helps lower cholesterol and protect cardiovascular health</td><td>Rich in antioxidants like anthocyanins and melanin, beneficial for anti-aging and beauty</td><td>Each has advantages based on health needs</td></tr></tbody></table><p>As illustrated, unshelled white sesame and unshelled black sesame each have unique benefits and suitable scenarios, making it difficult to determine which is better overall. The choice largely depends on personal taste preferences, cooking needs, and health goals.</p><p>If you prefer a refreshing taste and plan to use it for baking or stir-frying, unshelled white sesame may be more suitable for you. Conversely, if you enjoy a richer flavor and want to make traditional dishes like black sesame paste, unshelled black sesame may better meet your needs.</p><p>In terms of health benefits, both types have their own strengths. If you are focused on cardiovascular health and wish to lower cholesterol, unshelled white sesame might be more appropriate. However, if you are looking for anti-aging and beauty benefits, unshelled black sesame may have the edge.</p><p>Therefore, I believe that when choosing between unshelled white sesame and unshelled black sesame, one should weigh their actual situation and needs.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Which_is_Better_for_Industrial_Production_of_Sesame_Oil_Hulled_Sesame_Seeds_vs_Unhulled_Sesame_Seeds"></span>Which is Better for Industrial Production of Sesame Oil: Hulled Sesame Seeds vs Unhulled Sesame Seeds?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>In the industrial production of sesame oil, the choice between shelled and unshelled sesame primarily depends on production processes and cost-effectiveness.</p><ul><li><strong>Shelled Sesame:</strong> Shelled sesame requires shell removal before pressing, which increases production costs and complexity. However, some traditional methods claim that oil extracted from shelled sesame has a richer flavor and aroma. Additionally, the shell can generate some heat and pressure during pressing, aiding in extracting more oil and flavor compounds.</li><li><strong>Unshelled Sesame:</strong> Unshelled sesame skips the shelling step, reducing production costs and complexity. It also offers more uniform and stable pressing, which helps improve production efficiency and product quality. Furthermore, the oil extracted from unshelled sesame may have superior color, clarity, and taste. The removed shells can also be used for other purposes, such as soil amendments and organic fertilizers, significantly enhancing the economic value of sesame.</li></ul><p>Considering these factors, there is no absolute advantage or disadvantage in choosing shelled or unshelled sesame for the industrial production of sesame oil. It mainly depends on production processes, cost-effectiveness, and market demand. Some premium brands or traditional methods may prefer shelled sesame for its richer flavor and aroma, while large-scale, efficient production lines may lean towards unshelled sesame to lower costs and enhance efficiency.</p><p>Through this comparative analysis, we can see that both shelled and unshelled sesame have their merits. The choice largely depends on your usage scenario, personal preferences, and health needs. Whether shelled or unshelled, sesame is a nutritious and delicious ingredient with wide applications in cooking and health.</p>`} />
